  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAs of 145 AM Thursday morning, midlevel ridging continues to \nreside across the western half of the CONUS with troughing \nacross the east. Scattered thunderstorms have started to develop\nacross portions of central KS and within an axis of 850-700 mb \nWAA and on the edge of a 7-7.5 C/km midlevel lapse rate plume. \nThe WAA will continue to overspread north-central KS and areas \ngenerally along and east of I- 135. While severe weather is not \nexpected, PW values are forecast to increase to near 2\". Mean \nstorm motions have a component orthogonal to the WAA axis and \nmay yield a 1-2 hour period of training convection, mainly \nacross Lincoln, Ellsworth, Saline, McPherson, and Marion \ncounties. Should training of convection evolve, localized \nrainfall amounts in excess of 1\" would become possible. The \nbroad WAA pattern is expected to persist into the afternoon, \nagain for areas generally along and east of I-135. This should \nlinger showers/thunderstorms and cloud debris into the \nafternoon. Significant temperature modifications have been made \nfor high temperatures this afternoon due to that. Afternoon high\ntemperatures now expected to remain in the 80s for most \nlocations. In fact, the latest HREF produces 100% probabilities \nfor temperatures remaining below 85 degrees across the Flint \nHills. These probabilities decrease towards the I-135 corridor \nto around 50%. \n\nAnother complex of thunderstorm activity is likely late tonight into \nFriday. A shortwave trough is forecast to eject into the central \nPlains this evening into tonight. Scattered thunderstorm \ndevelopment is expected along an inverted trough axis across \neastern CO/western KS/western NE. As the evening progresses, \nconvection is likely to grow upscale into an MCS and translate \neastward within a 35-45 kt LLJ. Similar to this morning, a high \nPW environment (2-2.25\") will support heavy rainfall. Continued\nWAA into the daytime hours of Friday will likely result in \ncontinue scattered showers/storms and cloud debris. As such, \nhave trended high temperatures towards short range guidance \nwith highs remaining in the 80s across the Flint Hills to the \n90s for areas along/west of I-135. Additional storms are \npossible Friday afternoon into Saturday morning as the surface \ntrough slides across southeast KS. The core of the midlevel \nridge appears to slide back over the central Plains by next \nweekend. This will likely yield warmer temperatures, heat \nindices above 100 degrees, and minimal rain chances from \nSaturday afternoon into next week.",
